This is the strip @Vaibhaav-Tiwari flagged, and the width really doesn't shrink on collapse.

`--ao-sidebar-w` is set on `:root` by `useResizable` (Sidebar.tsx:187) and tracks only the **resize-drag** width. Collapsing never writes to it — the collapsed rail uses a separate token, `--size-sidebar-icon: 48px`, wired through `--sidebar-width-icon` on the provider. So this stays at the expanded width regardless of collapse state.

Working the numbers out of `tokens.css`:

| | value |
|---|---|
| strip width | `--ao-sidebar-w`, default **240px**, up to **420px** after a resize |
| collapsed sidebar | `--size-sidebar-icon`, **48px** |
| strip height | `--size-traffic-light-clearance`, **36px** |
| panel surface top edge | **14px** (`--size-center-panel-inset`; `<main>` carries no macOS top pad) |

So when collapsed there's an invisible `WebkitAppRegion: drag` region of roughly **192 × 22px** sitting over the top-left of the center panel surface — and up to **372px wide** for anyone who has widened their sidebar. On macOS that's exactly where this PR relocates the crumb and the New task / Orchestrator / bell controls, so the top slice of that row swallows clicks into a window drag.

The fix is slightly more involved than swapping the class, because this JSX lives in the component that *renders* `SidebarProvider` — so it's outside the provider's React context and can't call `useSidebar()` here. It needs to move into a child:

```tsx
function MacWindowDragStrip() {
	const { state } = useSidebar();
	return (
		<div
			aria-hidden="true"
			className={cn(
				"fixed top-0 left-0 z-chrome h-traffic-light-clearance",
				state === "collapsed"
					? "w-(--size-sidebar-icon)"
					: "w-(--ao-sidebar-w,var(--size-sidebar-default))",
			)}
			style={{ WebkitAppRegion: "drag" } as CSSProperties}
		/>
	);
}
```

then render `<MacWindowDragStrip />` in place of this block. `useSidebar` is already exported from `ui/sidebar.tsx` and exposes `state: "expanded" | "collapsed"`, so nothing else has to change.

Worth a small unit test asserting the width class flips with collapse state — this is invisible by construction, so a regression here would only ever be found by someone reporting that clicks mysteriously don't work.

