**[High] Capture-phase `stopPropagation()` silently kills every anchor's own click handler**

`document.addEventListener("click", handler, true)` fires before React's root-container listeners, so `stopPropagation()` here means no anchor's React `onClick` ever sees the event.

I verified this rather than pattern-matching it. Temporary probe added to the shell suite:

```tsx
it("PROBE: does an anchor's own click handler still run?", async () => {
  vi.spyOn(apiClient, "POST").mockResolvedValue({} as never);
  await renderShell();
  const card = document.body.appendChild(document.createElement("div"));
  card.dataset.sessionId = "sess-1";
  const anchor = card.appendChild(Object.assign(document.createElement("a"), { href: "https://github.com/o/r/pull/1" }));
  const own = vi.fn();
  anchor.addEventListener("click", own);
  fireEvent.click(anchor);
  expect(own).toHaveBeenCalled();
});
```

Result: `AssertionError: expected "vi.fn()" to be called at least once`.

Concrete regression: `NotificationCenter.tsx:369-374`. That PR-notification anchor's `onClick` runs `event.preventDefault(); onOpenPrimary(notification)`, and `openPrimary` (`NotificationCenter.tsx:65-75`) fires `captureRendererEvent("ao.renderer.notification_opened")` plus `window.open(prUrl)`, with `openAndDismiss` (line 148) closing the panel. The notification bell lives in the topbar, so on any session route a session is in scope and this handler wins: no telemetry event, the panel stays open, and the PR loads into the session Browser panel instead of the OS browser. No existing test catches it, because `NotificationCenter` tests render the component without the shell.

Suggested fix: drop `stopPropagation()` (`preventDefault()` alone already suppresses navigation) and bail early on `if (event.defaultPrevented) return;`, or register on the bubble phase so element handlers run first.

**[Medium] `new URL(anchor.href)` is unguarded and runs before the cheap checks**

`closest<HTMLAnchorElement>("a[href]")` also matches SVG `<a>` elements, whose `href` is an `SVGAnimatedString`, not a string. `new URL()` then throws inside a document-level capture listener. The `TerminalPane.tsx:290-295` equivalent wraps the identical call in `try`/`catch` for exactly this reason.

Two changes: move the `event.button !== 0` test above this line (it is the cheapest bail), and wrap the parse:

```ts
let url: URL;
try {
  url = new URL(anchor.href);
} catch {
  return;
}
```

