`lastActivityNanos` holds a wall-clock reading while the timer that consumes it is monotonic, so a backwards wall-clock step suspends the watchdog for the size of the step.

When the stored timestamp is ahead of `time.Now()`, `idle` is negative, the `idle < timeout` branch is taken, and the reset becomes `timeout - idle`, which is longer than `timeout`. Every later tick recomputes the same way, so a 30 minute backwards NTP correction means no stall row for the next 30 minutes, which is precisely the window this exists to cover. Nothing self-corrects until wall time catches up.

Reproduced on 4081a3c: with `idleActivityTimeout` at 20ms and `lastActivityNanos` set 2s ahead, which is the state a backwards step leaves behind, no stall surfaced across 20 idle windows.

Anchoring both ends to the monotonic clock closes it, and keeps all four watchdog tests green under `-race -count=2`:

```go
// package level
var watchdogEpoch = time.Now()

// noteActivity
c.lastActivityNanos.Store(int64(time.Since(watchdogEpoch)))

// tick
idle := time.Since(watchdogEpoch) - time.Duration(c.lastActivityNanos.Load())
```

A `if idle < 0 { idle = timeout }` clamp also closes this direction, but leaves the forward case, where a large wall-clock correction trips a stall row on a healthy connection.

