This flips to `"app"` on the **first successful poll even when there's no preview**. `getPreview` returns `null` when nothing has been generated, and `loaded` still becomes `{ id, value: null }`. The Browser pane keeps rendering because `!preview` is true, but `active` is now silently `"app"`. When the agent later generates a page, the next poll makes `preview` non-null and the screen **switches from the live browser to the app preview mid-use**, with no tap from the user.

It's also a side effect inside a state updater (StrictMode double-invokes updaters; harmless here only because `setActive` is idempotent).

Suggestion: track the default once, outside the updater, and only when a preview actually exists and the user hasn't picked a tab:
```tsx
const userPickedTab = useRef(false);
// in refreshPreview, after setLoaded(...):
if (value && !userPickedTab.current) setActive("app");
// in the PreviewTab onPress handlers: userPickedTab.current = true;
```
(and reset `userPickedTab` when `sessionID` changes).

One case is still open here, and it's my fault: the snippet I suggested last round had the same hole. When there's **no preview yet**, the tab switcher isn't rendered, so the user can't set `userPickedTab`. They're using the live Browser pane, the agent then generates a page, the next poll returns a value, and this line still switches them to App preview mid-use.

Decide the default only once, from the first poll result, and never auto-switch after that:
```tsx
const defaultDecided = useRef(false);
// in refreshPreview, after setLoaded(...):
if (!defaultDecided.current) {
  defaultDecided.current = true;
  if (value && !userPickedTab.current) setActive("app");
}
// reset defaultDecided alongside userPickedTab in the [sessionID] effect
```
Optional: render a short loading state until that first poll resolves. Right now `BrowserLivePane` mounts, opens a WebSocket (desktop starts a capture), and is unmounted a moment later when the first poll flips to App preview. That's a wasted start/stop, and it can race the exclusive lease if the user immediately taps back to Browser.
